        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Tours</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">10 October 732 (a Saturday in October, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicles record it)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Somewhere between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in northern Aquitaine, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; most historians place it where the rivers Clain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> join, but the exact location is unclear</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitanians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, Mayor of the Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, against the Umayyad army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>; numbers are unknown and modern estimates vary widely (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> 20,000 to 80,000; Franks 15,000 to 30,000 or more)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Abd al-Rahman was killed and the Umayyad army withdrew south; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> emerged strengthened and Duke Odo weakened.</span></div>
