    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The battle followed two decades of Umayyad conquest from 711, and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo of Aquitaine</a>, beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> for help.</li><li>Its details, including the numbers and the exact place, are unclear from the surviving sources, and the casualty figures later chroniclers gave are doubtful.</li><li>Historians disagree about its weight: some call it a turning point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, others a raid in force that has been turned into a myth.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The huge Umayyad losses of up to 375,000 that later writers gave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> are the same figures the Liber Pontificalis recorded for Odo's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 721.</p></aside>
    </section>

