    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">711</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> invade the Visigothic Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">721</span><span class="rf-text">Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo of Aquitaine</a> breaks the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and Al-Samh ibn Malik is mortally wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">730</span><span class="rf-text">Odo allies with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> commander Uthman ibn Naissa, who revolts against his Arab masters the next year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">732</span><span class="rf-text">Abd al-Rahman defeats Odo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> and plunders Bordeaux</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">732</span><span class="rf-text">Odo appeals to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, who agrees to help once Odo accepts Frankish authority</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">732</span><span class="rf-text">On 10 October, after about a week of skirmishing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> withstand the Umayyad cavalry and Abd al-Rahman is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">735</span><span class="rf-text">Uqba ibn al-Hajjaj invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">759</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> forces the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, ending Umayyad control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-martel.jpg" alt="Charles Martel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Martel_divise_le_royaume_entre_P%C3%A9pin_et_Carloman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Battle of Tours followed two decades of Umayyad conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that began in 711 with the invasion of the Visigothic Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> and were followed by expeditions into the Frankish territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> by 719, and Al-Samh ibn Malik al-Khawlani made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> his capital from 720. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo the Great</a> of Aquitaine broke the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 721 by surprising the Umayyad forces, and Al-Samh was mortally wounded, but raiding continued, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 725. In 730 Odo allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> commander Uthman ibn Naissa, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and gave him his daughter Lampagie in marriage. The next year Uthman killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Urgell and broke with his Arab masters, and Abd al-Rahman sent an expedition to crush the revolt before turning on Odo.</p><p>Odo gathered his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> but was defeated, and Bordeaux was plundered. The Chronicle of 754 said that at the fight on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> “God alone knows the number of the slain”. Odo's troops, like other European troops of the time, had no stirrups and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, and the Umayyad heavy cavalry broke his infantry in its first charge. Odo then appealed to the Frankish leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, who granted help only after Odo agreed to submit to Frankish authority. One possible motive for the Umayyad advance north, according to the second continuator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a>'s chronicle, was the wealth of the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Saint Martin of Tours</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tours&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Umayyads, Umayyad conquests from Hispania, Umayyad advance towards the Loire:</p><ul><li>Paul K. Davis, 100 Decisive Battles From Ancient Times to the Present (ABC-CLIO, 1999)</li><li>Roger Collins, The Arab Conquest of Spain: 710–797 (Blackwell, 1989)</li><li>Paul Fouracre, The Age of Charles Martel (Longman, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> was, at the time, perhaps the foremost military power in the world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</a> was its governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. His army had outpaced its supply wagons and split into raiding parties while the main body advanced more slowly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>'s Frankish realm was the foremost military power of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His Austrasian army consisted almost entirely of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripuarian-franks">Rhineland Franks</a> with large auxiliary forces from east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, among them Thuringians, Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>. He also sent word to the Neustrians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, though many Burgundian nobles did not take part, and Frisians were mustered too. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> apparently fought without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, and a core of seasoned infantry had fought under Charles for years.</p><p>The number of troops on each side is not known. The Mozarabic Chronicle of 754 says the Austrasians were greater in number, but virtually all Western sources estimate the Franks at 30,000, less than half the Muslim force. Edward Creasy, from later Muslim sources, gives the Umayyads as 80,000 or more. Paul K. Davis puts the Umayyads at 80,000 and the Franks at about 30,000, while noting that modern estimates run from 20,000 to 80,000. Edward J. Schoenfeld calls estimates of over fifty thousand Umayyad troops logistically impossible, and Victor Davis Hanson believes both armies were roughly equal at between 20,000 and 30,000. Gore places the Franks at 15,000 to 20,000 and the Muslims at 20,000 to 25,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles</a> marched south avoiding the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>, hoping to surprise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, and by all accounts they were caught off guard to find a large force in their path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. He chose not to attack but to draw up a defensive formation on high ground, described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>-like; according to Arab sources the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> formed a large square, with hills and trees in front to break up cavalry charges. For about a week the armies skirmished while the Umayyads waited for their full strength to arrive. The battle began on the seventh day, because Abd al-Rahman, with winter approaching, did not want to wait longer.</p><p>Abd al-Rahman trusted in his cavalry and had it charge repeatedly through the day. The Frankish infantry withstood the charges, though according to Arab sources the cavalry broke into the Frankish square several times without breaking it. The Mozarabic Chronicle of 754 says the northern peoples “remained as immobile as a wall” and that the Austrasians killed the king, Abd al-Rahman, striking him on the chest. Night fell and the Franks sheathed their swords until morning. At dawn they found the Arab tents still standing but empty, because the Umayyad army had left silently in the night. Charles Oman wrote that there was no pursuit, as Charles would not let his men leave the line.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short.jpg" alt="Pepin the Short" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PippinImperialChronicleCorpusChristiCollegeMS373Fol14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abd al-Rahman was killed and the Umayyad army retreated south over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Losses are unknown. Later chroniclers claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>'s force lost about 1,500 and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> suffered up to 375,000 dead, but the same figures were recorded in the Liber Pontificalis for Odo's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 721, and later writers, probably influenced by the Continuations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a>, attributed them to Charles alone. The Vita Pardulfi reports that Abd al-Rahman's forces burned and looted their way through the Limousin on their way home, which suggests that they were not destroyed as the Continuations imply.</p><p>Charles went on to extend his power in the south. After Odo's death, about 735, the Aquitanian nobility proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunald-i-of-aquitaine">Hunald</a> duke, and Charles confirmed his duchy once Hunald acknowledged him as overlord. In 735 Uqba ibn al-Hajjaj, the new governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and held part of Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> for four years. Charles invaded Septimania in 736 and 739 and destroyed an army that tried to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> at the River Berre, but failed to take Narbonne in 737. The Umayyads held parts of Septimania for another 27 years, until Charles's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> forced Narbonne's surrender in 759.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tours&#8221; cites where it covers Umayyad retreat and second invasion:</p><ul><li>Antonio Santosuosso, Barbarians, Marauders, and Infidels (Westview Press, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bede"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bede.jpg" alt="Bede" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-codices_bke-0047_001v_medium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">e-codices.unifr.ch, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The surviving sources say little. Few Arab authors refer to the battle, and allusions simply say that Abd al-Rahman and his companions found martyrdom; the Moroccan writer Ibn Idhari, in the 13th century, called the place the path of the martyrs, though Henry Coppée pointed out that the same name was given to the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and to other fields where Muslims were defeated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources of the 8th and 9th centuries are more numerous but imprecise: most chronicles report the event briefly, saying that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles</a> fought the Saracens on a Saturday in October. The Mozarabic Chronicle of 754, a contemporary Latin source, describes it in greater detail than any other Latin or Arabic source.</p><p>The Continuations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a> were composed by Charles Martel's family, and the Latin word they use for their warrior comes from the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>. A passage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> may also refer to the battle. The numbers of combatants and the exact location are unclear, and the battlefield lies somewhere between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. Ninth-century chroniclers treated the outcome as a divine judgment in Charles's favour and gave him the nickname Martel, the hammer, without tying it to one battle. The casualty figures later given for Tours are those recorded for Toulouse, and modern estimates of army size rest on what the countryside could support, which may be more accurate than the medieval figures.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tours&#8221; cites where it covers Contemporary accounts, Strategic analysis:</p><ul><li>Paul Fouracre, The Age of Charles Martel (Longman, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon.jpg" alt="Edward Gibbon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Gibbon_by_Henry_Walton_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Henry Walton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians fall broadly into two camps. One, beginning with the Mozarabic Chronicle and the Continuations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a> and carried on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a>, treats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> as a turning point. Gibbon wrote that the Umayyad advance, had it continued, might have carried the Saracens to the mouth of the Thames, and Hallam, Ranke, Delbrück and Kurth also gave it world importance. William E. Watson holds that it had macrohistorical significance but analyses it militarily, culturally and politically rather than as a simple Muslim against Christian confrontation. It laid foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, and Paul Davis says the victory ensured that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> would dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> for more than a century.</p><p>The other camp holds that the battle has been overstated. Alessandro Barbero says historians now play down its importance, since the Muslim force aimed to pillage the monastery of Saint-Martin, not to conquer the Frankish kingdom. Tomaž Mastnak calls the saving of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> a myth built by modern historians, noting that the Fredegar continuators saw one episode among many in Frankish wars for booty and territory. Philip Hitti writes that nothing was decided at Tours, because the Muslim wave had reached a natural limit, and Cowley and Parker say it has been downgraded to a raid in force. Bernard Lewis and Gustave von Grünebaum say Arab historians treat it as minor, while Khalid Blankinship counts it among the defeats that led to the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s decline.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Tours&#8221; cites where it covers Supporting the significance of Tours as a world-altering event, Objecting to the significance of Tours as a world-altering event:</p><ul><li>Paul K. Davis, 100 Decisive Battles From Ancient Times to the Present (ABC-CLIO, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> in October 732 a Frankish and Aquitanian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> withstood the cavalry of the Umayyad governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</a>, who was killed. Historians still disagree whether it was a turning point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> or a raid in force whose importance has been overstated.</em></p>

